WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.610 --> 00:00:05.430
Star Wars: The Rise of Skywalker has the unenviable
task of wrapping up the latest Star Wars﻿

00:00:05.430 --> 00:00:10.400
trilogy, uniting a fanbase that's become increasingly
divided, and concluding the decades-spanning,

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:16.240
so-called Skywalker saga. That's a lot for
one film to manage. Does it pull it off? Well…yes

00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:16.760
and no.

00:00:17.560 --> 00:00:19.520
The Rise of Skywalker should've been Leia's

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:24.120
movie. Han Solo got the spotlight in The Force
Awakens, and Luke Skywalker's story dominated

00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:29.410
The Last Jedi. With The Rise of Skywalker,
it was Leia's turn. Unfortunately, Carrie

00:00:29.410 --> 00:00:34.580
Fisher's tragic death in December 2016 made
a big Leia showcase impossible, forcing J.

00:00:34.580 --> 00:00:39.300
J. Abrams to cobble together an arc for the
princess-turned-general from old outtakes.

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:44.500
It's not perfect. Leia's dialogue is noticeably
vague, and it's obvious that Abrams is using

00:00:44.510 --> 00:00:49.130
a stand-in when Leia's face is hidden. She
doesn't have any standout moments, the climax

00:00:49.130 --> 00:00:53.260
of her arc is a little vague, and much of
her plotline is carried by other people talking

00:00:53.260 --> 00:00:57.780
about her, not what she actually does.
However, it works pretty well, and it's much

00:00:57.780 --> 00:01:02.560
bigger than a cameo. Leia is in multiple scenes,
and she has a complete arc in the movie. Obviously,

00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:06.680
it would've been better if Carrie Fisher were
still around to give Leia the send-off she

00:01:06.680 --> 00:01:10.790
deserves. Given what Abrams had to work with,
though, Leia's final on-screen appearance

00:01:10.790 --> 00:01:12.960
is significantly better than we expected.

00:01:13.420 --> 00:01:17.860
The last entry in the Skywalker saga begins
at a breakneck pace, and it doesn't slow down

00:01:17.860 --> 00:01:22.080
to breathe until the film's final minutes.
In order to set up its endgame, The Rise of

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:27.270
Skywalker introduces new characters, new planets,
new gizmos, and new ideas so fast that it's

00:01:27.270 --> 00:01:31.939
impossible to keep track of them all. Every
new plot thread introduces a dozen questions

00:01:31.940 --> 00:01:34.540
that Episode IX simply doesn't have time to
answer.

00:01:34.820 --> 00:01:39.030
While reports differ depending on who you're
talking to, it seems pretty clear that Disney

00:01:39.030 --> 00:01:43.380
and Lucasfilm didn't plan this new Star Wars
trilogy out from the beginning. In The Rise

00:01:43.380 --> 00:01:47.640
of Skywalker, that decision catches up with
them. The first half of the movie, while full

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:51.659
of action, is mostly set-up for the second
half. It would've been a lot better if some

00:01:51.659 --> 00:01:55.810
of those ideas that suddenly show up in Episode
IX had been seeded throughout the previous

00:01:55.810 --> 00:02:04.250
two movies.
"I have been every voice…you have ever heard…inside

00:02:04.250 --> 00:02:08.220
your head."
As it is, however, one film has to do all

00:02:08.220 --> 00:02:13.060
of the heavy lifting, and there's not quite
enough room. Returning characters like R2-D2

00:02:13.060 --> 00:02:18.810
and Rose Tico barely make an impact. New characters,
like Dominic Monaghan's Beaumont Kin and Naomi

00:02:18.810 --> 00:02:24.170
Ackie's Jannah, hardly register at all.
It all makes for a very bloated and unsatisfying

00:02:24.170 --> 00:02:28.840
viewing experience. There's a version of this
movie that's much tighter, but, unfortunately,

00:02:28.840 --> 00:02:30.240
it's not the one we got.

00:02:30.720 --> 00:02:35.500
You know who steals practically every single
scene he's in in The Rise of Skywalker? C-3PO.

00:02:36.180 --> 00:02:40.920
"Oh, they fly now!"
For one, Luke's fussy protocol droid is funnier

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:45.500
in Episode IX than he's ever been. But he
also proves surprisingly vital to the movie's

00:02:45.500 --> 00:02:51.400
story. Even in A New Hope, C-3PO is mostly
around to translate for R2-D2. This time,

00:02:51.410 --> 00:02:55.510
C-3PO's translation skills are instrumental
in moving the plot forward. If you told us

00:02:55.510 --> 00:03:00.230
a few years ago that Episode IX would have
us tearing up over C-3PO, a lifelong comedic

00:03:00.230 --> 00:03:03.740
relief character, we wouldn't have believed
you. And yet, here we are.

00:03:04.240 --> 00:03:06.900
Did you like The Last Jedi? If so, sorry.

00:03:07.260 --> 00:03:11.460
The Rise of Skywalker does a very good job
dismantling pretty much everything that movie

00:03:11.460 --> 00:03:16.720
put down. Supreme Leader Snoke's early demise?
As it turns out, he wasn't really that important.

00:03:17.080 --> 00:03:20.910
The near-extinction of the Resistance? It's
only temporary. When we rejoin them, they've

00:03:20.910 --> 00:03:25.880
found a lot of new recruits. Rey's parents?
We'll put it this way: They were definitely

00:03:25.880 --> 00:03:28.850
somebody.
The Rise of Skywalker buries its predecessor

00:03:28.850 --> 00:03:33.700
in a way that feels petty, sacrificing lots
of setup in unsatisfying fashion. The only

00:03:33.900 --> 00:03:36.920
thing from Episode VIII it really keeps are
the Porgs.

00:03:37.320 --> 00:03:39.620
Finn, Rey, Poe, and Kylo Ren are the best

00:03:39.620 --> 00:03:43.800
things about the new Star Wars trilogy. Even
when the script lets them down, these actors

00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:48.140
are so charismatic that it's fun just to watch
them run around, quip, bicker, and get into

00:03:48.140 --> 00:03:51.140
trouble.
The Rise of Skywalker gives us a lot of that,

00:03:51.140 --> 00:03:55.150
and it's just as fun as you'd expect, even
though Finn still doesn't have much of an

00:03:55.150 --> 00:03:57.030
arc.
Ultimately, though, the movie hinges on the

00:03:57.030 --> 00:04:01.510
relationship between Rey and Kylo. Thankfully,
Ridley and Driver are more than up to the

00:04:01.510 --> 00:04:06.200
task. The chemistry that the duo shared in
The Last Jedi is cranked up to 11 here, and

00:04:06.200 --> 00:04:10.960
the two actors carry the movie. Given how
bonkers the plot is, that's a massive feat.

00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:13.440
Going into The Rise of Skywalker, fans had

00:04:13.450 --> 00:04:18.400
some major questions. Now that it's out, they
still do. We never learn how Palpatine came

00:04:18.409 --> 00:04:23.759
back to life, he just…is. Rey's true nature
comes out of nowhere and isn't adequately

00:04:23.759 --> 00:04:28.550
explained, and many key plot points are glossed
over completely. The movie constantly tells

00:04:28.550 --> 00:04:31.600
us that things happened, but it doesn't tell
us how or why.

00:04:32.060 --> 00:04:34.600
You may recall that The Force Awakens did
the same thing.

00:04:34.940 --> 00:04:40.140
"Where'd you get that?"
"A good question, for another time."

00:04:41.420 --> 00:04:45.759
Granted, some of the Disney-produced spin-off
materials offer a few clues. But viewers shouldn't

00:04:45.759 --> 00:04:49.699
have to do extra research to understand the
basic plot of a movie, no matter how much

00:04:49.700 --> 00:04:51.580
Disney wants that to be the case.
Best: A fitting conclusion

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:56.830
J. J. Abrams went big with The Rise of Skywalker,
and if he didn't quite stick the landing,

00:04:56.830 --> 00:05:01.360
he certainly at least tried. It's a movie
that's full of spectacle. The dogfights, the

00:05:01.360 --> 00:05:04.949
lightsaber duels, and the blaster battles
are bigger than anything we've ever seen in

00:05:04.949 --> 00:05:09.689
a Star Wars movie before. And the Easter eggs,
cameos, and nods to past Star Wars films pop

00:05:09.689 --> 00:05:12.500
up everywhere.
It's hard to think of a better tribute to

00:05:12.500 --> 00:05:17.000
Star Wars, especially given the final scene,
which takes us back to where everything began.

00:05:17.560 --> 00:05:22.930
The Rise of Skywalker is bold, ambitious,
convoluted, messy, hopeful, overwrought, and

00:05:22.930 --> 00:05:25.740
complicated. In other words, that's Star Wars
in a nutshell.

00:05:26.200 --> 00:05:30.260
This is it. Luke, Leia, Han, C-3PO, R2-D2,

00:05:30.270 --> 00:05:34.539
and Darth Vader are done. For now at least,
it appears we finally know how this story

00:05:34.539 --> 00:05:37.629
ends.
The same seems to be true for our new heroes,

00:05:37.629 --> 00:05:41.749
too. None of the main actors involved with
the sequel trilogy seem particularly keen

00:05:41.749 --> 00:05:45.719
on coming back for more, and given The Rise
of Skywalker's definitive ending, it's hard

00:05:45.719 --> 00:05:49.969
to imagine that their future adventures will
be quite as exciting as the one that just

00:05:49.969 --> 00:05:53.259
concluded.
True, Star Wars isn't going anywhere. Disney

00:05:53.259 --> 00:05:58.110
will crank out new material set in a galaxy
far, far away for decades to come. Still,

00:05:58.110 --> 00:06:02.440
it's just not going to be the same without
our old (and newer) friends at the helm. Thanks

00:06:02.440 --> 00:06:04.960
for the memories, folks. May the Force be
with you.

